"Regarding al-Tin (the Fig) and al-Zaytun (the Olive), commentators have put forward two main views. According to the first view, what is meant here are the well-known fruits, the fig and the olive. Those who maintain this view state that figs and olives provide immense benefits for human life in terms of food, fruit, medicine, and trade, and that an oath may therefore have been sworn by these blessed fruits. Some, on the other hand, have stated that these two fruits symbolize the male and the female.\n\nThe second view is that Tin and Zaytun are proper nouns and point to blessed places. Those who prefer this view cite as evidence their mention alongside Tur-i Sinin (Mount Sinai) and al-Balad al-Amin (the Secure City) in the verse. Three different interpretations have been made regarding the nature of these places: mountains, mosques, or towns. For instance, it has been said that Tin is the mountain of Damascus or the mosque of Noah, while Zaytun is the mountain or the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis (Jerusalem). Furthermore, according to a narration from Ibn Abbas, Tin and Zaytun mean 'Mount Tin' and 'Mount Olive' in Syriac, and these mountains are the lands where Jesus and other prophets were raised.\n\nTur-i Sinin is the blessed mountain upon which Moses spoke with Allah. 'Tur' means a mountain covered with vegetation. It has been narrated that the word 'Sinin' signifies beautiful or blessed, and that it carries the meaning of beautiful in the Ethiopic language. Some scholars have explained the expression 'Tur-i Sinin' as 'the blessed, beautiful mountain,' stating that this designation is in harmony with such Quranic expressions as 'the Sacred Valley' (al-Wadi al-Muqaddas) and 'the blessed spot.'\n\nAl-Balad al-Amin, on the other hand, has been interpreted by consensus as Mecca. Mecca being designated as the 'secure city' rests on such reasons as Allah making the Kaaba a safe sanctuary for mankind, making it abundant and blessed upon the prayer of Abraham, and its being protected from chastisement due to the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) within it. These oaths express an ascent from material nourishment toward spiritual security, underscoring the vital importance of security for humanity.\n\nFollowing the oaths, Allah proclaims that He created the human being in the finest form. 'Ahsen-i takvim' (the finest composition) signifies that man was created in the best configuration, both physically and spiritually. This encompasses not merely outward physical beauty, but also spiritual capacities such as intellect, discernment, and moral aptitude. The human being possesses the potential to attain this sublime state. Yet, despite this initial creation, man can in time decline to the lowest of the low; this may take the form of bodily deterioration, moral degeneration, or plunging into Hellfire.\n\nHowever, those who believe and perform righteous deeds are saved from this degradation. Their reward is uninterrupted, and they attain the most excellent outcome. This constitutes the essence of religion: that Allah creates the human being in the finest form, after which one either preserves and elevates this beauty through faith and righteous action, or sinks into degradation through disbelief. The true meaning of religion is epitomized in this law of reward and punishment.\n\nFollowing these explanations, the verse asks: 'What, then, causes you to deny the religion?' In other words, in the face of such clear proofs and with Allah's promise and retribution so manifest, how can a person possibly deny religion or call it a lie? This question serves as a warning directed both to the human being himself and to the Prophet.\n\nIn the final verse, it is declared: 'Is not Allah the most just of judges?' He is above all sovereigns, ruling with absolute justice. Hence, His religion is the truth; He will grant reward to the believers and inflict punishment upon the disbelievers. Man must not forget this reality, must not give the lie to religion, and ought to attain eternal salvation through deeds that earn divine reward. This surah stands as a summary of the preceding surahs and a preparation for those that follow."